**Scenario:** "Design a checkout that supports credit card, PayPal, and wallet payments, with more providers coming later." What varies is the **behavior** of `pay()` — same call site, different algorithm per provider. That's a [Strategy](strategy-pattern) situation.

```java
interface PaymentStrategy {
    boolean pay(Order order);
}

class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy { /* ... */ }
class PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy { /* ... */ }

class Checkout {
    private final PaymentStrategy strategy;
    Checkout(PaymentStrategy strategy) { this.strategy = strategy; }
    boolean complete(Order order) { return strategy.pay(order); }
}
```

`Checkout` never grows an `if/else` per provider — a new payment method is a new class, not a new branch.

**Scenario:** "Users can opt into SMS, email, or push notifications, and can change their preference at runtime." Same shape as payments: the *behavior* of `send()` varies, and it must be swappable **while the app is running** — a second signal pointing at Strategy, not a compile-time choice.

```java
interface NotificationChannel {
    void send(String userId, String message);
}

class NotificationService {
    private NotificationChannel channel;
    void setChannel(NotificationChannel c) { this.channel = c; }
    void notify(String userId, String msg) { channel.send(userId, msg); }
}
```

Recognizing that two superficially different scenarios (payments, notifications) share one shape is exactly the pattern-matching skill this question tests.
